module v2.workflow.floor_non_verdict

import v2.std.algebra { Cons, Empty }
import v2.std.collection { List }

// THE NON-VERDICT ROSTER: identities enrolled as expected-red that the required floor EXECUTES
// and that produce NO BOOLEAN VERDICT -- they throw, or answer with something that is not a
// Bool -- enrolled by exact qualified name.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS, and why it gates rather than merely reports. `cli_run` `run_required_floor`
// routes such a claim to `known_red_runtime_errored` or `known_red_observation_unreadable`, and
// those arms are HONEST: they say correctly that this enrolled claim produced no verdict. The
// defect was in the COMPOSITION -- `claim_executor` `required_floor_outcome_is_clean` consulted
// neither, so the floor returned CLEAN while an enrolled expected-red assertion had ceased to
// assert anything. A diagnostic can describe evidence truthfully while the gate draws a false
// conclusion from it; that happened on every run for a day.
//
// THE RUNG, PER SEAM, because the governing rung is the minimum across them and stating only the
// strongest is the inflation DESIGN section 4b(1) forbids: routing a thrown claim into its arm is
// MITIGATABLE; printing the count is MITIGATABLE; printing `failed=0` without distinguishing
// verdict incompleteness is a MISLEADING PROJECTION; returning CLEAN while an enrolled claim
// produced no verdict is BELOW FLOOR. Below floor is not a rung -- it is outside the ladder and
// forbidden outright -- which is why this roster gates rather than waiting for the population to
// be repaid first.
//
// WHAT ENROLMENT ADMITS, AND WHAT IT CANNOT. Enrolment in `floor_expected_red` admits a known
// SEMANTIC VERDICT: this witness reaches its subject and answers false. It is not permission for
// the subject to stop evaluating. Without this roster the expected-red arm absorbs an arbitrary
// regression INSIDE the enrolled population -- yesterday the witness returned false, today it
// throws before its assertion, no verdict exists, and the floor stays clean. (Strictly inside it:
// an UNENROLLED witness that throws takes the ordinary failure path and already gates. The
// absorption was never repo-wide; saying otherwise would overstate the defect this closes.)
//
// WHAT THIS WALL CONFINES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Stated first because the short name for this
// module -- a growth freeze -- claims more than the mechanism delivers: growth is NOT refused in
// one direction.
//
// CONFINED, by execution, every run: the OBSERVED population must equal the ROSTER exactly. An
// observed identity the roster does not carry refuses (unrostered debt); a rostered identity the
// run did not observe refuses (stale exemption). Together those pin O = R.
//
// NOT CONFINED: the roster itself. Nothing compares the roster authored at HEAD against the
// roster admitted at the MERGE BASE, so an author whose change makes a NEW identity stop
// producing a verdict may add its row IN THE SAME CHANGE. Observed equals roster, both arms pass,
// and the regression is admitted -- LEGALISED BY THE ROSTER THAT EXISTS TO BOUND IT. The row
// becomes the permission, exactly the defect review 55361 identified for stale rows, arriving
// through authorship rather than survival.
//
// THIS IS A CONSEQUENCE OF THE SINGLE-RUN DESIGN AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT IN IT. The admission was
// specified as `added = head_observed - base_observed`, which needs a SECOND floor run over the
// base. Substituting the roster for base-observation makes the wall implementable in one run --
// sound exactly to the degree the roster cannot be grown freely. So `R subset-of B` is the
// condition that makes the simplification safe, not a nicety on top, and its absence is the
// honest cost of the cheap design.
//
// RUNG ON THAT AXIS: MITIGATABLE. Roster growth is confined by review diligence alone, strictly
// weaker than execution; this paragraph is the declaration, not a reader's inference.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the floor establishes `H subset-of B` -- the roster in the COMPOSED RESULT is
// a subset of the roster at that result's CURRENT TARGET-BASE PARENT. NOT the merge base, and the
// difference is a real admission: with M = {a} at the merge base, main repairing `a` and deleting
// its row (B = {}), and a stale branch reintroducing it (H = O = {a}), a subset-of-M rule PASSES
// and readmits an exemption main already retired. The mirror is a false red on a row main
// legitimately added after the fork. The cut therefore needs the composed result and its base
// parent, which a branch-head-only run cannot supply.

// THE ADMISSION AS IMPLEMENTED: the ADDED set is empty.
//
//     added  = head_non_verdict_identities - roster
//     repaid = roster - head_non_verdict_identities
//     admit  <=> added is empty
//
// Shrinking the observed identity set toward empty is admitted; adding any identity refuses. So
// does one old identity repairing while a different one begins throwing, even at equal
// cardinality -- THAT is why both sides are compared as IDENTITY SETS, never counts. A repaired
// witness must not buy permission for an unrelated witness to lose its verdict. The corpus paid
// for this lesson once in the other direction: the false regression that produced this module
// was a comparison whose `planned` counts matched on both sides while the trees did not.
//
// REPAYMENT AND ROSTER DELETION ARE ONE ACT, and an earlier revision of this header said the
// opposite: that refusing a stale row "punishes the fix", because gunbc#9020 repairs 99 of these
// in one landing and would go red for it. Review 55361 found why that is wrong: A STALE ROW IS A
// LIVE EXEMPTION. The identity is repaired, the row stays, and if that witness later stops
// producing a verdict it is ALREADY ROSTERED -- the exact regression this wall refuses, admitted
// in silence. Repayment without deletion turns a bounded debt row into a permanent licence: the
// absorbing fallback wearing the word "diagnostic".
//
// Refusing does not punish a repair; it requires the repair to be COMPLETE. The run names every
// row to delete, the deletion is mechanical, and `floor_route_gap` and the expected-red staleness
// join already enforce this discipline. The asymmetry was the error and consistency with them is
// the repair -- recorded because the asymmetry had an argument behind it and was endorsed before
// a reviewer found the hole.
//
// EMPTY IS THE INTENDED TERMINAL STATE. When every enrolled identity reaches its subject and
// answers, this module deletes, and the two collections become ordinary conjuncts over nothing.
//
// THE LIVE POPULATION IS ZERO AS OF 2026-08-26, WHICH IS THIS ROSTER'S DECLARED TERMINAL STATE
// AND NOT A HOLE IN IT. Eleven identities stood here -- two test.claim.host_phase_status, six
// test.claim.srv3_subsumption, two v2.test.claim.staging and
// v2.test.emit.produced_decl_support_preserved -- and every one now PRODUCES A VERDICT and passes,
// measured by the required floor. They retired together by one cause: the local-binding-shadow
// repair, which made a call site spelling a name held by both a local binding and a module-level
// free function reach the LOCAL. The displacement handed `atom_identity_hash` an argument of the
// wrong type and a record field lookup an Int, so these claims THREW before their assertions --
// exactly the state this roster keeps from being absorbed, and why eleven shared one root nobody
// could see from any individual row.
//
// THE ROWS ARE DELETED AND THE WITNESSES ARE NOT. A non-verdict row admits that an enrolled claim
// stops asserting; it is not an admission about the claim's subject. All eleven remain in the
// ordinary witness roster, now as executing evidence rather than declared debt.
//
// THE MODULE IS KEPT WITH AN EMPTY ROSTER RATHER THAN DELETED: the wall it composes into is what
// refuses a future non-verdict row that is unreachable, and deleting the module to celebrate a
// zero would remove the mechanism with its population. Reaching the terminal state is not the
// same event as dissolving the machinery; conflating them is the climb that deletes its own
// evidence (DESIGN 4b(4)).
//
// THE PRIOR POPULATION NOTE READ 15 IDENTITIES AND MAIN'S ROSTER HELD 11 WHEN this repair found
// it. That drift is recorded rather than quietly corrected: a transcribed count beside a list is
// the second representation that rots without anyone touching either end. The count above is
// derived from the rows this edit removed, and is zero because the rows are gone, not because
// anyone counted them.
//
// HISTORICAL: THE LIVE POPULATION WAS 15 IDENTITIES. The original 142-row roster was measured
// before #9039 repaired 127 identities, but #9095 authored that stale population after #9039 was
// already in its ancestry. The wall then proved those 127 rows unreachable: absent from
// `floor_expected_red`, they could never enter either known-red non-verdict arm. Retiring the
// exact non-verdict-minus-expected-red set keeps the 15 identities that can classify live debt;
// enrolling the other 127 as expected-red instead would fabricate semantic verdicts.
// The composed #9106 branch briefly carried a twelfth identity,
// gunbc.test.claim.realization_attempt_keystone_test.witness_planted_minimal_module_emits -- the
// same ClosureDependentResolution render-shadow specimen, repaid under #9259 before merge; its
// witness remains executing while its non-verdict and expected-red rows retire here.
fn floor_non_verdict_roster() -> List<String> {
  Empty {}
}
